// Homepage hero artwork: a thin-line contour animal that reflects the market regime —
// a bull while the Layer-1 market trends up, a brown bear while it trends down. The mood
// is computed server-side (see @/lib/market-mood).
//
// Both animals are machine traces of reference images, produced by the same pipeline:
// blur -> Sobel -> non-maximum suppression -> hysteresis leaves one-pixel ridges, those
// ridges are walked into polylines (following the straightest continuation, so eight-
// connected staircases do not shatter a line into crumbs), simplified with
// Douglas-Peucker, and emitted as strokes. Stroking rather than filling traced outlines
// is what keeps the line weight a single tunable number instead of a property of the
// raster; both are tuned to land at the same on-screen weight.
//
// The bull carries one extra pass: his reference sits on a white backdrop, where the pale
// horns barely differ from the paper, so their outer edge produces almost no gradient and
// Canny drops it — losing a whole horn. Flood-filling the backdrop from the frame edge
// and adding the subject's boundary ring restores the outline regardless of contrast.
//
// Background chains are dropped by position: anything below the animal's feet, or centred
// outside the box spanned by the ten longest chains, is snow ridge or stock-photo
// watermark rather than animal.
//
// pathLength="1" lets one CSS keyframe draw the whole figure on regardless of its real
// length; the animation is dropped under prefers-reduced-motion.

